WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.700
Every time Netflix adds another tween dramedy
to their service, you have to wonder: where

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:05.769
are all the good movies?

00:00:05.769 --> 00:00:08.300
Well, they're there — they just take some
digging.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.940
From chilling horror films to quirky comedies,
Netflix is filled with great movie selections

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.120
if you know where to look.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.510
Here's a roundup of some of the best Netflix
hidden gems you probably haven't seen yet.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.029
In Order of Disappearance

00:00:20.029 --> 00:00:23.359
You'll be rooting for the underdog in this
hard-boiled crime thriller straight from the

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:25.109
snowy streets of Norway.

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:29.730
Starring Stellan Skarsgard and Game of Thrones'
Kristofer Hivju, In Order of Disappearance

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:34.290
follows a quiet, seemingly average snow plow
operator on a quest to avenge his the murder

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.290
of his son.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:39.610
Equal parts pitch-black comedy and explosive
revenge thriller, In Order of Disappearance

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.010
is as unstoppable as its protagonist.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.230
As the New York Times' review put it, it's
somewhere between Fargo and Quentin Tarantino,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.280
and the end result is way more fun than it
should be.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.560
"Hello?"

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.330
"Well, he can't, he can't actually talk right
now.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.240
Because he's done."

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:58.960
Officer Downe

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.680
Let's just preface this by saying that you
shouldn't expect a lot from Officer Downe.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.540
Based on the 2010 graphic novel, the movie
is a hyper-stylized splatter-fest about a

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:08.790
cop who can't die.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:09.790
Well, he can.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.549
He just comes back to life again.

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:13.299
That's about it as far as plot goes.

00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:17.281
There's a criminal organization that dresses
like animals, some gun-toting nuns, and a

00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:21.869
clan of ninja-type people, and as you'd expect,
they all take turns trading blows with undead

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:23.479
supercop Officer Downe.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.600
Fortunately, the movie doesn't ask or expect
the viewer to take any of this seriously.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.390
It doesn't even ask you to care all that much
about who these people are — all it really

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.369
wants is to have gunfights and explosions,
and in that respect, it does okay.

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:38.670
Just turn off your brain and let Downe take
care of the rest.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.819
Ava's Possessions

00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:44.490
Just when you thought the demonic possession
genre had been exorcised of all traces of

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:49.219
originality, along comes Ava's Possessions,
a twist on the trope that looks at life after

00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:50.619
a possession happens.

00:01:50.619 --> 00:01:54.450
It's a fresh take on the whole possession
thing, drawing a satirical parallel between

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.320
people who've been possessed and alcoholics,
support groups and all.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.540
"My name's Ava"

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.259
"My uninvited spiritual guest possessed me
for 28 days"

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:05.450
"And was exorcised on Tuesday."

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.750
If you're looking for something out of the
ordinary to spend an evening with—or just

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.420
in the mood to find some bleak comedy in the
unlikeliest of places—you could definitely

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.260
do a whole lot worse.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.300
War on Everyone

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.250
For all of its unlikable characters, War on
Everyone is a surprisingly enjoyable movie.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:24.610
The main characters are Terry and Bob — played
by Alexander Skarsgård and Michael Peña,

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:28.060
respectively — two crooked cops with absolutely
no moral code.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.120
Instead of stopping crimes, they spend their
days blackmailing criminals in exchange for

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.120
kickbacks.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.850
This is technically a buddy cop comedy, but
the black humor and overall message that nothing

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.130
really matters take it to some weird places.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:43.090
And yet somehow, by the time the last bullets
have found their mark, you realize that you

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.190
kind of like these two amoral sleazebags who
haven't performed one single act of redemption

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:48.320
the whole movie.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.840
War on Everyone won't hit home with everybody,
but if you like dark humor, you may find yourself

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.810
enjoying it a little more than you should.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:55.810
Moon

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:01.370
You may know Duncan Jones as a director from
recent films like Mute or Warcraft, but in

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.660
2009, Jones released his debut film Moon,
a low budget sci-fi gem that pulled in less

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.120
than $10 million at the box office.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.120
Starring Sam Rockwell and a robot who sounds
like Kevin Spacey, it's the definition of

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.740
a bottle movie — just one man in a lunar
research station, slowly losing his mind.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.370
"You guys been to any...good parties or anything?"

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.210
Of course, any trailer will tell you that
there's a little more to it than that, but

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.510
we're not trying to spoil anything here.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:30.430
If you've never seen Moon, do yourself a favor
and avoid all the marketing and just queue

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.930
it up on Netflix.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:32.930
A Perfect Day

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:36.120
It was a simple job: just get the corpse out
of the well.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.400
But nothing is ever simple for this group
of aid workers during the Yugoslav Wars.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.820
Beset on all sides by uptight U.N. bureaucrats,
gun-toting kids, and booby trapped cows, all

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.380
they want to do is find a piece of rope so
they can pull a body out of a local town's

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.180
water supply before it poisons the villagers.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:57.570
With a cast led by Benicio del Toro, Tim Robbins,
and Melanie Thierry, A Perfect Day is both

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:02.790
an absurd comedy of errors and a low-key satire
of the regulations and red tape involved in

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.360
international conflict.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.351
Without making any grand statements, the movie
suggests a parallel between the main characters'

00:04:08.351 --> 00:04:11.080
small-scale mission and the larger surrounding
war.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.670
If it's this hard to find a rope, how is an
entire country supposed to figure out its

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:16.670
problems?

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:17.670
Blame!

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.229
There seem to be two camps when it comes to
this Netflix animated film — those who like

00:04:21.229 --> 00:04:25.840
the original manga hate the movie, and those
who haven't read the manga generally enjoyed

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:26.840
it.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.210
But love it or hate it, when it comes to fresh
takes on the apocalypse, Blame! has most any

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.409
other futuristic vision beat, hands down.

00:04:32.409 --> 00:04:34.509
That's what makes this film so intriguing.

00:04:34.509 --> 00:04:38.690
In a seemingly endless, continually growing
city that goes for miles up, down, and in

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:42.249
either direction, robots have taken to exterminating
humans.

00:04:42.249 --> 00:04:46.210
But a small band of people has survived for
centuries inside an invisible shield that

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:47.560
keeps the robots out.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.480
They don't know why the robots can't get inside
their colony; they just know that going outside

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.720
means risking life and limb at the hands of
predatory robots.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.169
It's only when a stranger comes to their town
that they realize there may be a chance to

00:04:58.169 --> 00:05:02.199
do something about their predicament and finally
stop the expansion of the city.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:05.719
For vision alone, Blame! is well worth watching.

00:05:05.719 --> 00:05:06.719
The Vault

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:10.800
James Franco headlines The Vault as the assistant
manager of a bank that gets knocked over by

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.129
a group of petty thieves.

00:05:12.129 --> 00:05:14.129
The twist is, the bank is haunted.

00:05:14.129 --> 00:05:17.780
When Franco leads this heist gang down into
the underground vault, they get more than

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:18.300
money.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:18.940
They get ghosts.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.810
You won't be rocking back and forth in terror
from The Vault, although it does have its

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:29.810
chilling moments.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:34.090
You won't end up rooting for the thieves,
although it does open them up to empathy eventually.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:38.569
And you definitely won't be chuckling at James
Franco's silly antics, because he's a stone-faced,

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:41.599
mustachioed man on a mission once things really
get rolling.

00:05:41.599 --> 00:05:46.022
What The Vault lacks in such departments as
characterization, script, and suspense, it

00:05:46.022 --> 00:05:47.720
more than makes up for in ghosts.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:48.919
Bunches of ghosts.

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:52.030
If you love ghosts, you'll easily tolerate
The Vault.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:53.479
Cold in July

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:57.809
Starring Michael C. Hall and Sam Shepard,
Cold in July begins with a quiet family man

00:05:57.809 --> 00:05:59.599
who shoots a burglar in his house.

00:05:59.599 --> 00:06:03.759
Soon, though, his family is being terrorized
by the departed young man's angry father,

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:05.910
who's fresh out of prison and out for revenge.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:10.129
While that alone would make for an interesting,
if predictable film, Cold in July quickly

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:14.069
switches gears as these two men realize that
there are bigger stakes involved than their

00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:15.539
petty revenge tale.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:19.150
One of Cold in July's biggest strengths is
that you never guess where the story is going

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:20.150
next.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.449
Each new twisted development takes the characters
in an entirely new direction.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:26.979
If there's a downside to the whole thing,
it's that the ending is over a little too

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:27.979
quickly.

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:31.419
But it's not enough to ruin what's otherwise
a solid, engaging movie.

00:06:31.419 --> 00:06:32.419
Oculus

00:06:32.419 --> 00:06:37.389
Netflix has a million and a half horror movies,
and the vast majority of them aren't exactly

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:38.840
gems, hidden or otherwise.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.310
Oculus looks a lot like the rest of them when
you're scrolling through Netflix, but with

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:45.740
one teensy, important difference — it's
actually pretty great.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:49.500
A brother and sister return to the house where
their father murdered their mother, bringing

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.749
along the haunted mirror that made him do
it.

00:06:51.749 --> 00:06:56.050
Their plan is to record themselves being driven
crazy by the mirror so the whole world knows

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:59.740
that an evil mirror is responsible for that
horrible crime back in the day.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:03.639
Even the description sounds a little silly,
but Oculus is that one rare gem that pulls

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:04.160
it off.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.910
Oculus was one of writer and director Mike
Flanagan's first films with a decent budget,

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:17.449
and it shows in every frame.

00:07:17.449 --> 00:07:21.370
Flanagan has since gone on to direct several
other films, including one of those Ouija

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:26.990
movies that were everywhere in 2016 and Netflix's
Gerald's Game adaptation, so his is definitely

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.180
a name you'll be hearing about more often
in the future.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.669
Fullmetal Alchemist

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:34.870
Is the live-action version of Fullmetal Alchemist
as good as the anime?

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:35.870
Not even close.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:38.559
Is it cheesy and hilarious and full of ridiculous
moments?

00:07:38.559 --> 00:07:39.689
Oh, you bet.

00:07:39.689 --> 00:07:43.919
For newcomers to the whole thing, the story
is about two brothers with a natural inclination

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:46.830
for alchemy who attempt to bring their mother
back to life.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.419
Things go horribly wrong, and one brother
ends up with his soul trapped in a suit of

00:07:50.419 --> 00:07:53.020
armor while the other loses an arm and a leg.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:57.180
When they're older, they search for the mythical
philosopher's stone in the hopes of restoring

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.180
their bodies.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:02.119
As far as anime adaptations go, Fullmetal
Alchemist is arguably one of the best out

00:08:02.119 --> 00:08:03.119
there.

00:08:03.119 --> 00:08:06.749
It doesn't cover the whole story, but it serves
as an origin tale and works as a self-contained

00:08:06.749 --> 00:08:09.039
story that even non-fans can enjoy.

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:12.999
At least, you can enjoy it if you already
like over-the-top action fantasies.

00:08:12.999 --> 00:08:14.759
That's all this is.

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:15.900
Super Dark Times

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:20.619
This coming-of-age story has all the staples
of the genre — high school friends, teenage

00:08:20.619 --> 00:08:23.370
drama, house parties, and pubescent angst.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.759
But for two best friends, a traumatic experience
may be too much for them to reconcile.

00:08:27.759 --> 00:08:32.649
In his feature-length debut, director Kevin
Phillips has crafted a tight, unique teenage

00:08:32.649 --> 00:08:33.649
thriller.

00:08:33.649 --> 00:08:37.680
The rich atmosphere and superb performances
from a relatively unknown cast add weight

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.479
to an already heavy script.

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:41.550
This isn't an easy movie to watch.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:45.850
Although other films may be more gratuitously
violent, Super Dark Times doesn't offer a

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:49.040
hint of escapism in its few brutal moments.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.920
If you're looking for lighthearted viewing,
you'd be better off queuing up something else.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.100
But if you want to experience a superb film
with a dramatic message, you could do worse

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:59.050
than Super Dark Times.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.620
A Serious Man

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:04.550
Even by Coen brothers standards, A Serious
Man is an unusual movie.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:09.360
It starts with a subtitled prologue set in
the 19th century and then moves into a suburb

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.750
in the Midwest circa the late '60s.

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:15.510
The main character is Larry Gopnik, a Jewish
physics professor.

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:17.920
"So, am I right?

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:21.840
Okay, so if that's that, then we can do this."

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:27.390
When Gopnik's life starts being derailed by
a chain of events completely beyond his control,

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:30.130
he visits three rabbis to figure out the meaning
of life.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:33.980
It's not the Coens' best movie by a long shot,
or even their best comedy.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:36.740
In the end, you'll probably come away scratching
your head.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:40.660
But maybe you'll also watch it again a year
later, because there's something there, something

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.019
you can't put your finger on.

00:09:42.019 --> 00:09:44.630
There has to be something holding it all together.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:47.520
Or maybe, just maybe, none of it means anything.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.860
"Okay - the uncertainty principle.

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:56.120
It proves we can't ever really know what's
going on."

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:57.279
Thanks for watching!

00:09:57.279 --> 00:10:00.250
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:02.570
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

